# Lab 2 — Web directory enumeration detection

## Goal

Stand up a web server on the host, point Wazuh at its access log, run a directory brute-forcing tool (`dirb`) against it, and confirm that Wazuh detects the scan.

## Environment

| Component | Detail |
|---|---|
| Host | Acer Nitro ANV15-51, Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, 16 GB RAM |
| SIEM | Wazuh 4.14 (all-in-one) |
| Web server | Apache 2 |
| Scanner | dirb 2.22 |

## Steps

1. Installed Apache and dirb, and created two "hidden" directories so the scan would have something to find:

   ```bash
   sudo apt install -y apache2 dirb
   sudo mkdir -p /var/www/html/admin /var/www/html/backup
   echo "secret admin page" | sudo tee /var/www/html/admin/index.html
   echo "backup files here"  | sudo tee /var/www/html/backup/index.html
   ```

2. Told Wazuh to monitor the Apache access log by adding this block to `/var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf`, just before `</ossec_config>`:

   ```xml
   <localfile>
     <log_format>apache</log_format>
     <location>/var/log/apache2/access.log</location>
   </localfile>
   ```

   Then restarted the manager: `sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager`.

3. Ran the scan against the host:

   ```bash
   dirb http://localhost/ /usr/share/dirb/wordlists/common.txt
   ```

   dirb sent 9,224 requests and found `/admin/`, `/backup/`, `/index.html`, and `/server-status`.

## Results

The scan generated **3,878 alerts** of rule **31101 – "Web server 400 error code"**, all within the same second, from a single source.

![Alert spike in Threat Hunting](images/web-scan-events.png)

Opening one alert shows the detail a Tier 1 analyst would use to investigate:

| Field | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `data.srcip` | `::1` | source of the scan (localhost) |
| `data.url` | `/admin/wp-admin` | a path the scanner probed (WordPress admin — not even installed here) |
| `data.id` | `404` | server response: not found |
| `rule.groups` | `web, accesslog, attack` | Wazuh classifies this as an attack |
| `rule.firedtimes` | `3,769` | how many times this rule fired — shows the scale |
| full_log user-agent | `Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1)` | dirb's default user-agent, a fake IE6 |

![Alert detail](images/web-scan-detail.png)

Wazuh also maps the alert to compliance controls: **PCI DSS 6.5 / 11.4**, **NIST 800-53 SA.11 / SI.4**, and **GDPR IV_35.7.d**.

### What gave the attack away

- One source produce nearly 3800 404 request that prove that it is not by a human but web scanning tool.
- A fake Internet Explorer 6 user-agent from 2001, a classic sign of an automated scanner rather than a real browser.
- Sequential, dictionary-style paths (`/admin/xxx`, `/admin/zipfiles`, `/admin/wp-admin`) instead of links a real user would follow.

### What I learned

- This is the same type of attacking method as Lab 1, brute-force, one is repeat requests to find web links, other is breaking password through many attempts.
- Wazuh can not detect the scan until i pointed it at Apache' logs, SIEM tool only detect what it usually collect.
- Wazuh ties technical alerts to compliance frameworks, which is how a single 404 becomes an audit-relevant event.

## Troubleshooting note — recovering a broken ossec.conf

While editing the config in `nano`, the file got overwritten with a stray line and the original was lost (no backup existed). Recovery:

```bash
sudo mv /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf.broken
sudo apt-get install --reinstall -o Dpkg::Options::="--force-confmiss" wazuh-manager
```

This restored the packaged default config without touching any alert data. After that I added the `<localfile>` block with a single `sed` command instead of a text editor, to avoid repeating the mistake:

```bash
sudo sed -i 's|</ossec_config>|  <localfile>\n    <log_format>apache</log_format>\n    <location>/var/log/apache2/access.log</location>\n  </localfile>\n</ossec_config>|' /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf
```

**Lesson:** back up config files before editing, and prefer a targeted command over hand-editing a large file.

